.nh
.TH "PVEFORGE" "1" "Sep 2026" "pveforge" "pveforge manual"

.SH NAME
pveforge-harness-build - Build the nested cluster's three outer VMs (690-692)


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBpveforge harness build <target-id> [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Create the three outer VMs of the nested cluster (690 pvh-n1, 691 pvh-n2,
692 pvh-nfs) on the harness node, through pveforge as the pool token, with the
full create-field matrix screened against lib.sh's allow-list. It checks a clean
slate first (nothing created unless all preconditions hold), then creates,
starts, waits for the installed systems, ejects the node ISOs, and pins the host
keys. On any failure it destroys only the VMs this run created (or keeps them
with --keep-on-failure). Site values come from ~/.config/pveforge/harness-build.env.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--created-file\fP=""
	path to this run's created-VM ledger (default $HARNESS_CREATED_FILE)

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for build

.PP
\fB--keep-on-failure\fP[=false]
	on failure, leave this run's VMs for inspection instead of destroying them

.PP
\fB--lock-wait\fP=0s
	how long to wait for another pveforge process's lock on this object before giving up (0 = the 15m0s default; at most 1h0m0s). The default exceeds the 10m0s ceiling on waiting for a PVE task because a legitimate lock holder may be in such a wait the whole time

.PP
\fB-o\fP, \fB--output\fP="kv"
	output format: "kv" or "json"

.PP
\fB--pool\fP="pveforge-harness"
	harness pool a VM must be a member of

.PP
\fB--repin\fP[=false]
	replace existing host-key pins for the three addresses (a new build makes new keys)

.PP
\fB--roster\fP=""
	path to the roster file (overrides PVEFORGE_ROSTER and the default ./pveforge.toml)

.PP
\fB--storage\fP=""
	the outer storage id (or $HARNESS_STORAGE)

.PP
\fB--tag\fP="pveforge-harness"
	harness tag a VM must carry


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBpveforge-harness(1)\fP
